<p>A <strong>relational database</strong> keeps an application’s data in tables that can be read together. A shop might keep customers in one table and orders in another. Each order records which customer placed it, so the application can find both the order and the person behind it.</p>

<p>Consider a shop selling tea and tea things. Mina Shah places an order, closes her browser and comes back tomorrow to check it. The shop needs to remember that order beyond the request that created it. Its database holds the record; the application reads it to show Mina her order history and reads the same record to show the shop what needs packing.</p>

<p>Keeping those facts useful takes more than saving them. The application needs a way to tell customers apart and connect each order to the right one. Start with how those facts fit into tables.</p>

<section aria-labelledby="tables-and-their-rows">
  <h2 id="tables-and-their-rows">Tables and their rows</h2>

  <p>A <strong>table</strong> is a named collection of rows and columns. In the shop’s <code>customers</code> table, each <strong>row</strong> describes one customer. Each <strong>column</strong> holds one kind of fact about every customer: <code>customer_id</code> holds their identifier and <code>name</code> holds their name.</p>

  <p>Read the outlined row from left to right.</p>

  <ShopTables />

  <p>The two values belong together: <code>1</code> identifies the customer whose name is Mina Shah. The row below says that customer <code>2</code> is Tariq Aziz. Adding another customer adds another row with values under those same column headings.</p>

  <p>The identifier is how the shop tells one customer from another. Two customers could have the same name, so a name alone would not tell an order which one it belongs to. Here the shop gives each customer a different <code>customer_id</code>.</p>

  <p>The <code>orders</code> table has one row per order. Its <code>order_id</code> identifies the order, while its <code>customer_id</code> identifies the customer who placed it. Follow order <code>103</code>: read its <code>customer_id</code>, then find that value in <code>customers</code>. Switch to order <code>102</code> and follow it the same way.</p>

  <ShopTables linked />

  <p>Order <code>103</code> carries customer <code>1</code>, which leads to Mina Shah. Order <code>101</code> carries <code>1</code> too: Mina has two orders, but still only one row in <code>customers</code>. Order <code>102</code> carries <code>2</code> and leads to Tariq Aziz. Edda Berg has a customer row without an order.</p>

  <p>It is the matching value that connects the rows. Order <code>103</code> is the third order drawn, but it belongs to customer <code>1</code>, not the third customer. The rows could be displayed in a different order and those connections would stay the same.</p>
</section>

